# The rows come from the streamreader, so this case needs neither a database nor a container
# runtime, and the "file system" is the local one: hdfswriter and hdfsreader both take
# `file:///` as their defaultFS, the way 070/080/090 do. That keeps the case runnable in CI,
# which has no cluster, while still exercising the readers end to end through the packaged
# distribution.
#
# What it adds over 080/090 (which round trip the scalar columns of a database fixture) is the
# column types those cases never write: a decimal, a date that is the epoch itself, a timestamp
# before the epoch, an array, a map, and a varchar and tinyint whose name the reader has to
# resolve. It also reads every file a second time with `column: ["*"]`, which takes the types
# from the schema of the file instead of the job configuration.
